The distributed binaries statically link a number of third-party Rust crates.
Third-party dependencies keep their own licences. The .deb carries the licence
text of every crate the binary links, with the crates each text covers, as
/usr/share/doc/district-ai/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt, beside this file. It is
written from Cargo.lock by cargo-about (about.toml and about.hbs in this
repository) when the package is built, and running scripts/build-deb.sh
writes the same file into target/release for a build of your own.

Third-party notices for native libraries bundled with a release (for example in
a Flatpak or a .deb) are listed in this file when such a library is added.

libwebrtc
---------

A build with calls (district-call's `livekit` feature, which the app's `voice`
feature turns on) statically links libwebrtc, as this project builds it for the
LiveKit Rust SDK: LiveKit's own recipe and pinned sources for release
webrtc-89d790b of github.com/livekit/rust-sdks, with every patch LiveKit
applies, built by scripts/build-libwebrtc.sh without the H.264 and H.265 codecs
or FFmpeg. The archive is webrtc-linux-x64-release.zip in release
libwebrtc-89d790b-audio-1 of this repository, SHA-256
2355bc8c6cdaf9613c471da944ac901bb293f346dda5e94cdbe6249a36914317, which is the pin in
scripts/fetch-libwebrtc.sh. The archive's LICENSE.md, which that script unpacks
beside the library, holds the full licence text of every component below, and a
distribution of such a build must ship it with the binary. The packages do: the
.deb as /usr/share/doc/district-ai/libwebrtc-LICENSE.md and the Flatpak as
/app/share/licenses/com.distronode.DistrictAI/libwebrtc-LICENSE.md, each beside
this file. The default build links none of this.

